// Expected: c0e0 at one session, c0e1 at two.
//
// The minimal statement of cross-session confusion. Alice seals a message
// under a long-term pre-shared key and nothing in the ciphertext says which
// session it belongs to.
//
// At one session the attacker can only corrupt the ciphertext, which fails
// Bob's checked AEAD_DEC and halts him. Bob then holds no plaintext at all,
// and an aborted principal has nothing to disagree with, so the equivalence
// query holds.
//
// At two sessions it holds a second ciphertext that decrypts perfectly under
// the very same psk. Feeding it to the first session's Bob passes his check
// and leaves him holding m#2 where Alice sent m: two honest parties, both
// following the protocol, ending up with different messages. `m` itself is
// never learned -- psk does not leak -- so confidentiality holds throughout.

attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private psk
	generates m
	e = AEAD_ENC(psk, m, nil)
]

Alice -> Bob: e

principal Bob[
	knows private psk
	d = AEAD_DEC(psk, e, nil)?
]

queries[
	confidentiality? m
	equivalence? m, d
]
